<p>After a long argument with Grammarly this morning, we decided to part ways for a bit. The issue started with Micro.blog&rsquo;s built-in CSS editor for my blog&rsquo;s template, essentially a text box containing my CSS.</p>
<p>Grammarly kept insisting on fixing my code. There&rsquo;s an option to tell it to ignore certain websites, and that was the first thing I tried, but it kept highlighting my CSS in angry red. It also tied up my memory and froze Safari whenever I tried to select all for copy-paste (I edit the code in Emacs, where I have syntax highlighting for CSS). I turned the extension off. Nothing. I got annoyed and uninstalled it… it was still there. I removed it from my system completely, including the additional desktop app they have (for applications like Outlook, which exist outside of the browser), and it was <em>still</em> there, still monitoring every keystroke. It only went away after a full restart.</p>
<p>I like what Grammarly does, and it&rsquo;s been <a href="https://taonaw.com/2024/12/30/my-writingblogging-environment.html">part of my workflow</a> for a while, but I&rsquo;m wondering if it&rsquo;s only because I&rsquo;m used to it now. Apple introduced its &ldquo;Apple Intelligence&rdquo; Writing Tools about two years ago, and I haven&rsquo;t really been using it. Meanwhile, I do <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/03/22/using-ai-to-edit-and.html">use AI to edit my post</a>, and it catches grammar issues and weird sentences just as well.</p>
<p>I much rather not have AI scan and read my texts except when I give it something I want to be public. The idea that I have a sort of key-logger on my system that reads every single sentence I write is creepy enough (I&rsquo;m glad it can&rsquo;t monitor Emacs…). I gave the built-in Writing Tools a run, and I think it&rsquo;s up for the job, so I&rsquo;m going to try them a bit longer and see if I miss Grammarly.</p>
